### What is this question really testing?

Estimation skill, honesty, and recovery. The interviewer wants to see that you recognize a bad estimate early, communicate quickly, re-plan sensibly, and improve your estimation process afterward.

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class EstimationTest {
    enum Signal { HONEST_IMPROVER, SILENT_HOPER }
    public static Signal evaluate(boolean detectedEarly, boolean communicated, boolean improved) {
        return (detectedEarly && communicated && improved) ? Signal.HONEST_IMPROVER : Signal.SILENT_HOPER;
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(evaluate(true, true, true)); // HONEST_IMPROVER
    }
}
```

### What was the underestimate?

Adding multi-currency support to the account service at National Bank of India. I estimated two days. It took far longer because of hidden complexity I had not considered.

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class TheUnderestimate {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Task: add multi-currency support");
        System.out.println("Estimate: 2 days");
        System.out.println("Actual: 2+ weeks");
        System.out.println("Hidden complexity: decimal rounding per currency, exchange rate storage, compliance checks");
    }
}
```

### How did you detect the gap early?

Within the first day of real work, it became clear the task was far bigger than two days. I did not quietly hope to catch up.

```java
package com.bank.service;
import java.util.logging.Logger;
public class EarlyDetection {
    private static final Logger log = Logger.getLogger(EarlyDetection.class.getName());
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int estimatedDays = 2;
        int discoveredSubtasks = 8; // rounding, rates, compliance, tests, migration...
        log.warning("Day 1: discovered " + discoveredSubtasks + " sub-tasks vs " + estimatedDays + " day estimate");
        log.info("Action: raised it immediately, did not wait for deadline");
    }
}
```

### How did you communicate and re-plan?

I went to my manager, explained the hidden requirements, and proposed delivering the most critical currency path first.

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class CommunicateAndReplan {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("To manager:");
        System.out.println("  Root cause: hidden complexity in rounding, rates, and compliance");
        System.out.println("  Revised plan: deliver INR + USD path first (critical)");
        System.out.println("  Defer: rare currencies to next sprint");
    }
}
```

### How did you improve your estimation process?

I started recording estimates versus actuals and calculated a historical optimism factor. Now when my gut says two days, I adjust by the factor before committing.

```java
package com.bank.service;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.math.RoundingMode;
public class EstimationCalibration {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        BigDecimal totalEstimated = new BigDecimal("40"); // hours across 10 tasks
        BigDecimal totalActual = new BigDecimal("64");
        BigDecimal optimismFactor = totalActual.divide(totalEstimated, 2, RoundingMode.HALF_EVEN);
        System.out.println("Optimism factor: " + optimismFactor);
        BigDecimal nextEstimate = new BigDecimal("16"); // gut says 16 hours
        BigDecimal calibrated = nextEstimate.multiply(optimismFactor).setScale(0, RoundingMode.HALF_EVEN);
        System.out.println("Gut estimate: " + nextEstimate + "h, Calibrated: " + calibrated + "h");
    }
}
```

```text
Optimism factor: 1.60
Gut estimate: 16h, Calibrated: 26h
```

### Why is hidden complexity the usual culprit?

Edge cases, integration points, and compliance requirements are invisible at first glance. A spike task to explore unknowns before committing prevents the worst surprises.

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class HiddenComplexity {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Visible: API endpoint for currency conversion");
        System.out.println("Hidden: exact decimal rounding per currency pair");
        System.out.println("Hidden: storing historical exchange rates for audit");
        System.out.println("Hidden: regulatory compliance checks for cross-currency");
        System.out.println("Fix: always add a spike task for unknown dependencies");
    }
}
```

### What is the lasting improvement?

Later estimates became dramatically more reliable because I calibrated with real data instead of trusting an optimistic gut.

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class LastingImprovement {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Before calibration: off by 60% on average");
        System.out.println("After calibration: off by 15% on average");
        System.out.println("Process: spike unknowns + calibration factor + daily tracking");
    }
}
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class EstimationFollowUps {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("How accurate now? -> Within 15% on average");
        System.out.println("What if you cannot spike? -> Add buffer explicitly and communicate the uncertainty");
        System.out.println("Team adopted? -> Yes, we all track estimate vs actual now");
    }
}
```
